WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:05.220
Poke a cell, and a current flows.

2
00:00:06.160 --> 00:00:09.020
In 2010, nobody knew which protein carried it.

3
00:00:09.760 --> 00:00:10.760
How would you find out?

4
00:00:11.650 --> 00:00:15.730
Coste and colleagues used a cultured mouse cell line called Neuro2A.

5
00:00:16.470 --> 00:00:18.350
It gave a reliable current when poked.

6
00:00:19.350 --> 00:00:21.570
A separate glass probe pressed on the cell.

7
00:00:22.370 --> 00:00:24.450
A patch pipette recorded the whole-cell current.

8
00:00:25.483 --> 00:00:29.803
They silenced candidate genes one at a time, with short interfering RNA.

9
00:00:30.783 --> 00:00:32.883
Then they asked which loss reduced the current.

10
00:00:33.883 --> 00:00:35.483
One candidate stood out.

11
00:00:36.343 --> 00:00:40.043
Knocking down a gene called Fam38A sharply reduced the current.

12
00:00:41.067 --> 00:00:43.127
The current shrinks when this gene is silenced.

13
00:00:44.067 --> 00:00:45.067
What have you shown?

14
00:00:45.607 --> 00:00:46.607
And what have you not shown?

15
00:00:52.617 --> 00:00:56.477
You have shown the gene is required, for that current, in that cell line.

16
00:00:57.217 --> 00:00:59.397
You have not shown its protein is the channel.

17
00:01:00.417 --> 00:01:03.517
A required protein could be an accessory subunit.

18
00:01:04.297 --> 00:01:06.257
The real pore could be made of something else.

19
00:01:07.250 --> 00:01:10.090
Next, they put the gene into other cultured cells.

20
00:01:10.910 --> 00:01:16.010
The renamed gene, Piezo1, gave those cells mechanically activated currents.

21
00:01:17.050 --> 00:01:19.310
So did a related gene, Piezo2.

22
00:01:20.330 --> 00:01:22.130
The two currents had different kinetics.

23
00:01:23.133 --> 00:01:28.333
Ion substitution pointed to a non-selective cation current for Piezo2.

24
00:01:28.333 --> 00:01:31.173
And its current inactivated quickly.

25
00:01:32.250 --> 00:01:36.170
Inactivation means the current decays while the stimulus is held.

26
00:01:37.110 --> 00:01:39.770
Keep that word. It returns at the end.

27
00:01:40.733 --> 00:01:44.893
Notice the logic. Silencing showed the gene is necessary.

28
00:01:46.113 --> 00:01:47.293
Expression showed it is sufficient.

29
00:01:48.353 --> 00:01:49.833
Neither showed that it forms the pore.

30
00:01:50.817 --> 00:01:52.137
The authors said so.

31
00:01:52.977 --> 00:01:57.797
They kept open the possibility that Piezo proteins were subunits of some other

32
00:01:57.797 --> 00:01:58.797
channel.

33
00:01:59.117 --> 00:02:03.117
Necessity, sufficiency and pore identity are three separate claims.

34
00:02:04.057 --> 00:02:05.397
Each needs its own experiment.

35
00:02:06.317 --> 00:02:07.737
The 2010 paper earned two.

36
00:02:08.717 --> 00:02:10.237
Later work earned the third.

37
00:02:11.177 --> 00:02:17.477
In 2012, purified Piezo1 formed ion channels in artificial bilayers and liposomes.

38
00:02:18.517 --> 00:02:20.217
Structures then placed the pore.

39
00:02:21.017 --> 00:02:24.697
The channel's carboxy-terminal domains line a central ion pore.

40
00:02:25.650 --> 00:02:27.270
So the protein is the channel.

41
00:02:28.270 --> 00:02:29.270
Now a stranger question.

42
00:02:30.090 --> 00:02:32.870
Why build so much protein around one central pore?

43
00:02:33.883 --> 00:02:36.263
Required, sufficient, and the pore itself.

44
00:02:37.163 --> 00:02:38.783
Three claims, three experiments.
